<!--
  Home Church, the two rows that keep Add to calendar from crashing on an
  old iPhone.

  THIS IS A FRAGMENT, NOT A FILE. Add both rows to the existing
  ios/App/App/Info.plist, or add them through Xcode's Info tab the way
  XCODE.md step 10 adds the export compliance row. It is kept here rather
  than as a whole Info.plist because that file is generated by
  `npx cap add ios` and carries a dozen keys Capacitor manages; replacing it
  wholesale would trade one bug for several.

  WHY THEY ARE NEEDED, AND WHY THEY WILL ALMOST NEVER BE SEEN. Add to
  calendar puts up EKEventEditViewController, which is the system's own New
  Event sheet. From iOS 17 that sheet needs no calendar permission and shows
  no prompt: the person is acting inside Apple's UI, and the app never gets
  access to anything. Nearly every phone this app runs on is iOS 17 or
  later, so nearly every phone will never read these strings.

  Below iOS 17 the same sheet does need access, and iOS asks for it by
  reading a purpose string out of Info.plist. With no string there, iOS does
  not show a prompt and does not refuse the request — it kills the app. A
  crash, on tap, on exactly the oldest phones in the church.

  So these are insurance, not a feature. They cost nothing on a modern
  phone and they are the difference between a prompt and a crash on an
  iPhone 8. scripts/preflight.js checks for the first one in ios/ when that
  directory exists, which is the only place it can be checked, because ios/
  is generated and gitignored.

  WRITE-ONLY IS THE HONEST ONE. The app adds an event and does not read the
  calendar, so iOS 17's write-only string is the accurate description of what
  it wants. NSCalendarsUsageDescription is the pre-17 key and has no
  write-only equivalent, so it says the same thing in the words that era
  understood.
-->
<key>NSCalendarsUsageDescription</key>
<string>Home Church adds a church event to your calendar when you ask it to. It never reads your calendar.</string>

<key>NSCalendarsWriteOnlyAccessUsageDescription</key>
<string>Home Church adds a church event to your calendar when you ask it to. It never reads your calendar.</string>
